The module deals with complex, space-related scientific and cultural theories related to landscape architecture at Master's level, which deal with special theories in landscape architecture, including: Design as a cultural technique, structuralism, minimalism, transparency, complexity, semiotics in landscape architecture, functionalism, atmospheres etc.. In accompanying, short design exercises (sketch problems), the focus is on in-depth examination of the lecture topics mentioned and discussion of the applicability of the theories in the landscape architectural analysis and design process with the aid of recommended specialist literature.

OBJECTIVES:
After successfully completing the module, students will be able to:
• apply the essential basic principles of complex, space-related scientific and cultural theories (e.g. structuralism) in the context of exemplary problems from current landscape architecture in design;
• emphasise the complex relationships between theoretical concepts from architecture, visual arts, urban planning and landscape architecture;
• describe exemplary interrelationships qualitatively;
• critically evaluate case studies;
• analyse complex problems of landscape architecture projects through individual examination of the theoretical concepts discussed and derive concrete spatial design solutions from this.

